  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">The Physics of the "Stack Effect"</h2>
    <p>
      In winter, heated air inside your house expands and rises toward the ceiling. This creates positive air pressure against the attic ceiling drywall. At the same time, cold air leaks in through basements and crawl spaces (negative pressure). This natural convection engine is called the <strong>stack effect</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      When ceiling drywall was installed, 1/4-inch gaps were left where drywall sheets meet 2x4 wooden wall partition top plates. In a standard 1,800 sq ft home, adding up all the top plate gaps, wire holes, plumbing vent chases, and electrical junction boxes equals a <strong>2-foot by 2-foot hole permanently open to the attic</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Why Insulation Doesn't Stop Airflow</h2>
    <p>
      Blown fiberglass is made of spun glass fibers. Blown cellulose is recycled shredded newspaper treated with fire retardants. Both materials work by trapping pockets of still air (conductive resistance). <strong>Neither material stops moving air.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Warm indoor air rises straight through the porous fibers into the unconditioned attic space. When that warm air hits cold plywood roof decking, moisture condenses, soaking your insulation and rotting the roof sheathing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The 4 Critical Penetrations to Seal</h2>
    <ol class="space-y-3 not-prose my-4">
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">1. Interior Partition Wall Top Plates:</strong>
        Pull back existing batt insulation and run a continuous bead of expanding polyurethane foam along both sides where ceiling drywall meets the 2x4 wood plate.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">2. Electrical Romex Wire Holes:</strong>
        Electricians drill 3/4-inch holes through top plates to drop Romex cable down to switches and receptacles. Fill every hole with expanding fireblock foam.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">3. Plumbing Vent Pipes &amp; Chases:</strong>
        Plumbing stacks leave 2-inch to 4-inch gaps through the ceiling. Bridge wide gaps with foam board or metal flashing, then seal perimeter with expanding foam.
      </li>
      <li class="p-3 bg-gray-50 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-xs text-gray-700">
        <strong class="text-gray-900 block text-sm mb-1">4. Recessed Can Lights:</strong>
        Older recessed can lights leak immense amounts of heat. Build 5/8\" drywall or rigid foil-faced polyiso boxes over non-IC rated fixtures, or swap them out for modern airtight LED retrofit wafers.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="Elena Rostova, CBO"
    role="Certified Building Official & Building Science Specialist"
    experience="18+ Years Field Experience"
